WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.697 --> 00:00:09.030
TIN-YUN HO: My name is Tin-Yun
and I'm a Product Manager

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.695
on the Cloud AI team.

00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:13.320
And today I'm here to talk about
a new product we are launching

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.020
for delivering highly
personalized shopping

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.595
experiences on GCP.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.860
But before we begin , I wanted
to step back and provide

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.570
a little bit of context.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.050
What is so hard about delivering
good recommendations, which

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.170
is the key component
of personalization?

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.870
Well, if you think
about the basic setup,

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.380
it involves, for a given user
in a context that they're

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.140
interacting with
UN, recommending

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.390
an item from a long
list of potential items

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.680
that you can show them.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.940
And there are challenges along
each of these major steps.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.040
So for users, you
have cold start users

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.763
that you've never seen before.

00:00:51.763 --> 00:00:53.430
You have users who
have shifting habits.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.600
So even if you have
seen them before,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.850
they may not be behaving
the same way anymore.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.310
And you also have the
sparse feedback problem.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.960
They don't actually
interact with every one

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:01.668
of your products.

00:01:01.668 --> 00:01:03.550
They only ever
interact with a few.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.440
And frequently only
the head items.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.240
Then you have changing context.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.400
They're on different
pages of your website.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.960
They're interacting with you
from a different device, iOS,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.488
or Android, or what not.

00:01:14.488 --> 00:01:15.530
There's changing channel.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.655
Sometimes they're in store.

00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:19.430
Sometime there's on
mobile versus desktop.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.140
And then there's
changing location.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.840
They might be signing in
from different geographies.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.690
And finally, items.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.380
You might have a
new product launch,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.650
so it's a cold start
item with no history.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.870
So you don't necessarily
know how to recommend it.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:34.190
You have sparse labels.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.260
So especially for
the tail items,

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.960
you might not have
significant signal on how

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.290
people click and purchase it.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:41.840
And it's hard to recommend.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.600
And then, especially
in the retail setting,

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.840
for a lot of your products,
the most important information

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.330
is not necessarily in
the clicks and purchases,

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:51.890
but in the
unstructured metadata.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.440
So for example, the
description of a book

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.810
or the image of a dress.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.720
And in this situation, with
all these different challenges,

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.120
you have to solve all of them to
deliver a good recommendation,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.290
which is actually really
difficult. And by the way,

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.050
you have to do this
in 100 milliseconds

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.350
with up to billions of items
and billions of users, at least

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.490
in the Google setting.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.530
So luckily here at
Google, we've been

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.510
able to accumulate
years of experience

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.880
across multiple
flagship properties

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.290
on how to make
recommendations better,

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.660
including with the latest
deep learning approaches.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.720
So for example, in Search, we do
personalized search re-ranking.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.020
With YouTube, we predict what
video you should watch next

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.970
based on your viewing history.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.090
In Google Play, we predict what
app you're likely to install,

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.840
and so forth.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.080
So here's an example
from YouTube.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.580
Now this was published
in the Verge.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:49.240
So more than 70% of the time
people spend watching videos

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.820
on the site is now
driven by YouTube's

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.980
algorithmic recommendations.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.860
That's a significant share.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.620
So in light of this,
both the challenges

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.600
as well as the great
recommendations technology

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.000
and infrastructure we've
built up at Google,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.500
we decided to
provide all of this

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.770
as a managed service to enable
retailers to deliver state

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.230
of the art, highly
personalized product

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:14.320
recommendations to their
shoppers in real time

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.730
and at scale.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.440
We call this Product
Recommendations

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.070
AI, which I'm proud to
announce is in limited beta,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.510
as of yesterday.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:28.020
The way it works is we provide
a fully managed global scale

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.960
service that basically
works out of the box.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.670
And by managed
service, I mean there's

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.695
a whole slew of problems
that we solve on your behalf

00:03:35.695 --> 00:03:37.570
so there's no need for
you to think about it.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.180
So for example,
there's no need for you

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.680
to think about cleaning
and pre-processing

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.300
your adjusted data
because we'll do

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.520
things like deduplicate
your user events, filter bot

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.672
traffic, et cetera,
on your behalf.

00:03:46.672 --> 00:03:48.630
No need to think about
writing state of the art

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.700
machine learning code,
keeping up with the latest

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.520
deep learning advances, making
sure that your models cover

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.430
your entire product catalog,
and do so in low latency.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.110
All these are very
challenging problems

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.270
and we take care of that
for you, essentially

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:03.480
cherry picking the best of
what is coming out internally

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.987
from the other consumer-facing
teams at Google.

00:04:05.987 --> 00:04:08.070
There's no need for you
to think about maintaining

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.930
resilient training pipelines.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.580
Making sure that your
pipelines run day

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.643
in and day out without failure.

00:04:13.643 --> 00:04:15.810
No need to think about
addressing common biases that

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:19.649
can skew your recommender, such
as popular items, seasonality,

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:21.570
on sale items, as
well as making sure

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:23.820
that your recommender properly
recommends when it hits

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.273
cold start items or tail items.

00:04:26.273 --> 00:04:27.690
No need to think
about replicating

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.140
your service across regions.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.120
We do that for you.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.850
So wherever your shoppers
are, we can serve it.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.830
No need to think about
provisioning servers

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.372
to handle spiky traffic.

00:04:36.372 --> 00:04:37.830
Retailers in the
room probably know

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.310
that you can have 3x or
even 10x spikes in terms

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.262
of user traffic.

00:04:42.262 --> 00:04:43.470
So no need to think about it.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:45.178
We'll automate dealing
with that for you.

00:04:45.178 --> 00:04:48.600
No need to think about building
monitoring, error checking,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.680
and learning systems, as
well as relevant dashboards.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.020
So obviously, this is
extremely important in case

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.100
your recommendation fails for
some reason in the pipeline.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.780
We'll warn you and
we'll also enable

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.215
you to check it and debug
it in your dashboard.

00:05:03.215 --> 00:05:04.590
And then finally,
no need for you

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.110
to manage the latency,
availability, and cost for all

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.730
of these above components.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.800
The way to think about
it is that not only are

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.190
each of these steps extremely
difficult to deal with,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.470
but there are also
potential points of failure

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.460
for your overall system.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.540
So we take care of all of
that, make it resilient,

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.400
make it work, make
it best practice,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.700
and all you need to do is ingest
your product catalog and user

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.960
events.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.560
And then from the Predict
API, retrieve and then display

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.240
the recommendation results.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.450
And then you only have to pay
for the recommendations served.

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.610
So all the underlying
infrastructure costs,

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.260
we take care for
you, we bundle it in.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.690
In terms of
recommendation options,

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:46.550
we provide a
comprehensive portfolio

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:50.030
of choices suited for the retail
setting, which you can retrieve

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.940
with just a simple Predict API
call, as I mentioned earlier.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.310
So first, you can choose
your recommendation type.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.100
And we have the
primary types that are

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.020
needed for a retail website.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.910
For example,
recommended for you,

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.220
which is based on your
shopping history, what are you

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:07.010
likely to click on or buy next?

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:08.510
Usually used on the home page.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.750
Other items you may
like for recommending

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.000
other relevant items on
the product detail page,

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.220
but doing so in a
personalized way,

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:17.810
based on your shopping history.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.160
Frequently bought together.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:22.040
So suggesting complimentary
items to a current one

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.530
that you're already
thinking of buying, as

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.300
opposed to a substitute item.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.470
And then shopping cart
and registry expansion.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:30.980
So let's say you already have
five items in your cart, what

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:34.910
other items go with that cart?

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:37.310
Next, for each of these
recommendation placements,

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:38.840
you can define your objective.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.510
So whether you want to
optimize for engagement

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:43.880
or click through rate
at the time, conversion

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.070
rate, or revenue procession.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:50.400
So we could re-rank the options
based on the price for you.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.100
And finally, fine
tuning results.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.990
So you can do things
like filtering out

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:56.153
of stock items, filtering
duplicate items,

00:06:56.153 --> 00:06:58.070
especially useful if you
have a large category

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.020
with many overlapping items.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.210
Filter by custom tags
that you provide.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:05.270
Turn diversification
on or off, as well as

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:07.610
turn personalization on or off.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:10.070
Usually personalization
will achieve better metrics,

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:12.200
but there might be unique
circumstances where

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.990
you don't want to personalize.

00:07:16.918 --> 00:07:19.210
And as I mentioned earlier,
as input to the recommender

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.790
system, we take your
product catalog, as well as

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:23.840
a live stream of
your user events.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.840
And because we're using
the latest deep learning

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:29.050
architectures, we put
all of your data to work.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:31.130
Every last ounce of it.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.550
So for example, in
the product catalog,

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.430
we not only use the history
of clicks and purchases

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.170
for each of those
products, but we also

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:40.990
use the rich metadata that's
available in the catalog you

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:41.800
provide.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.660
The name of the product, the
description, the categories,

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:45.380
et cetera.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.340
All of these things
are used directly

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.350
as inputs in the
neural net model

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.870
to enable our models to
recommend better, especially

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.240
for cold start
items or tail items,

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.330
where the only signal
we have potentially

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.860
is from the metadata itself.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.090
Next, we also take a live
feed of real time user events

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:05.470
and we use every bit of it.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:09.070
Not only just the aggregate
clicks and purchases, but also

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:11.320
the exact sequence
of user events,

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.423
down to what happened in the
last second in order to inform

00:08:15.423 --> 00:08:16.090
the recommender.

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:18.970
So the exact sequence,
the event type

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:21.070
of each of the elements
in the sequence,

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:24.040
the ordering, the distance
between each of the events.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.800
All of these are used as
signal into our model.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.810
As well as things like
the domain and device,

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:32.709
other custom features you might
provide for the event context.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:39.299
Ingesting this data
is easy as well.

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:43.049
So we have built in integrations
to enable easy data ingestion

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:45.210
from a large number
of touch points

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:47.080
that you already
have with Google.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:49.500
For example, if you already
send your product catalog

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.460
to the Google Shopping team for
managing your product listing

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.800
ads, then you can use
the same integration

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.420
for Recommendations AI.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.390
Similarly, if you already
use Google Tag Manager

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.007
for ingesting user
events from your website,

00:09:03.007 --> 00:09:05.340
then we do have a native tag
integration with Google Tag

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.458
Manager that you can turn on.

00:09:07.458 --> 00:09:09.000
And off course, if
your historic data

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.310
is stored in Google Cloud
Storage or BigQuery,

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.190
we can ingest from there as
well in order to, for example,

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:16.230
warm start the recommender.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:19.020
But of course, if you prefer
more traditional options

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.870
like a back end API or
a JavaScript snippet,

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:23.550
we have those options
available, also.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.220
And of course, none
of this would matter

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.795
if we couldn't deliver results.

00:09:29.795 --> 00:09:32.420
And I'm proud to say that, after
working with a number of pilot

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.270
customers all over the world,
delivering recommendations

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:38.510
on their full 100% production
e-commerce traffic,

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.010
we've been able to
prove our ability

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:44.420
to deliver significant lift
on key business metrics

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:47.900
that you care about, as shown
in AB experiments controlled

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:51.180
by our customers versus
their baseline solutions.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.350
So for example, up
to a 90% improvement

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:57.140
in recommendation-driven
click through rate.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:00.710
Up to a 50% improvement in
recommendation-driven revenue.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:05.210
As well as up to a 5%
improvement in total revenue

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:05.932
procession.

00:10:05.932 --> 00:10:07.640
I'm especially proud
of this last metric.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.800
It looks small, but actually
it's the biggest one.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:14.360
Because typically, for
the pilot customers

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.290
we worked with,
the baseline tends

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:20.930
to provide only a small
share of their total revenue.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.170
Something like 10%.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.210
We've even seen less than 5%.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.420
So if you can increase the
total revenue by up to 5%,

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.750
that's actually a pretty
significant improvement.

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:32.240
And as a caveat,
these metrics are

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:35.225
meant to give you a sense
of the upper ranges of what

00:10:35.225 --> 00:10:36.350
we've been able to achieve.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:37.730
The upper ranges.

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:40.370
The full range of actual
results can vary dramatically

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.662
based on a lot of factors,
such as the positioning

00:10:42.662 --> 00:10:44.120
of the recommender
on your website.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.050
If you put it at the
very bottom of the page,

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:48.300
obviously we're not going
to impact total revenue that

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.760
much no matter what.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.530
The number of products
in the catalog.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.020
The more products you
have, the more likely

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:56.420
a recommender has room to
do innovative new things

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:59.250
that it couldn't do before.

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:01.700
But whether there are any
data integration issues.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.310
And this is actually
the biggest issue

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:07.040
that we've come across when
it comes to model quality.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.540
If people can't
achieve the results,

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.820
typically there is some
kind of data integration

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:11.960
issue involved.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.610
Things like maybe
the user events

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:15.390
are not being sent properly.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:18.560
Maybe the product catalog is
not being updated regularly.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.320
Maybe the predictions
are not actually

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:23.330
being shown in the
appropriate order.

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:27.830
All of these are things that
we've hit on a regular basis

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:29.220
the middle of AB experiments.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.310
So this is probably
the biggest category

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.020
of things to watch out for.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.492
And finally, of course, if
you have a large data science

00:11:35.492 --> 00:11:37.700
team that has already been
working on recommendations

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:40.100
for many years, likely
you won't see these kinds

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.050
of results across the board.

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:45.410
But I believe that
it would be great

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.270
if you could test it
on individual locations

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.490
that maybe you haven't
worked as hard on.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:52.190
And then it could still provide
significant lift for those.

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:54.560
So you can think about ways
to mix and match whatever

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.093
is the best of breed from
in-house, as well as from us.

00:11:58.093 --> 00:11:59.760
That's one of the
reasons why we decided

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.177
to price per prediction, so
you can do that kind of thing.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.730
And now I'd like to show
